or floaties, are swim aids designed to help a wearer float in water and learn to swim."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Lucas_in_the_pool.jpg/330px-Lucas_in_the_pool.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inflatable_armbands","wikidata":"Q1457356"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Wing","blocks":[{"text":"A wing is a structure which produces both lift and drag while moving through air. Wings are defined by two shape characteristics, an airfoil section and a planform. Wing efficiency is expressed as lift-to-drag ratio, which compares the benefit of lift with the air resistance of a given wing shape, as it flies. Aerodynamics includes the study of wing performance in air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg/330px-Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing","wikidata":"Q161358"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Water-3D-balls-A.png/500px-Water-3D-balls-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ball-and-stick model of a water molecule","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater-3D-balls-A.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/1280px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/500px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A drop of water falling toward water in a glass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_drop_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co